---
/*
  The doorway: a section's own introduction, in Denvil's voice, at the top of
  its landing page.

  Not an article and not a panel. It is the paragraph a person reads before
  they decide whether this section is for them, so it gets the reading measure,
  the lead sentence at display size, and no card, border or box around it. The
  words come from a real, editable, linkable entry rather than being typed into
  a template, so changing them is a CMS edit and not a deploy.

  Two sources feed it, because the sections are not all the same shape. Essays,
  Moments and For Leaders are collections, so their doorway is the pageIntro
  post that already pins to the top of the list. Bookshelf and Studies are not
  collections, so theirs is a document in `landing`. Either way this component
  takes a rendered body and lays it out identically.

  It adapts to how full the room is, and this is the whole reason it was
  rewritten. Measured on 30 Aug, the doorway ran 540 to 1005 pixels tall and
  pushed the first real piece of content to 1320 on Essays and 1353 on
  Bookshelf: a screen and a half of scrolling before a visitor met a single
  post. Watch, which has no doorway at all, reached content at 402.

  So: while a room is empty the introduction is the only thing on the page and
  it is doing real work, so it prints whole. The moment the room has something
  in it the introduction folds down to its opening paragraph with a disclosure,
  because a person who came to read the posts should see the posts. Same words,
  same place, earning its height only when there is nothing else there.

  The fold is a details element and no JavaScript. The lead paragraph is split
  off the rendered body rather than typed twice, so there is still exactly one
  copy of the writing and it is still edited in the CMS.
*/
interface Props { tone?: 'blue' | 'yellow' | 'coral' | 'green'; collapsed?: boolean; label?: string }
const { tone = 'yellow', collapsed = false, label = 'Read the rest' } = Astro.props;
import Mark from './marks/Mark.astro';
import Tape from './marks/Tape.astro';
import DotGrid from './marks/DotGrid.astro';

const raw = (await Astro.slots.render('default')) ?? '';
const cut = raw.indexOf('</p>');
const lead = cut === -1 ? raw : raw.slice(0, cut + 4);
const rest = cut === -1 ? '' : raw.slice(cut + 4);
const folds = collapsed && rest.trim().length > 0;
const hasRail = Astro.slots.has('rail');
---
<section class:list={['shell', 'door', folds && 'is-folded']}>
  <div class="door-in">
    {folds ? (
      <>
        <div class="door-body"><Fragment set:html={lead} /></div>
        <details class="door-fold">
          <summary class="door-toggle">
            <span class="door-toggle-t">{label}</span>
            <span class="door-caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="door-body door-rest"><Fragment set:html={rest} /></div>
        </details>
      </>
    ) : (
      <div class="door-body"><Fragment set:html={raw} /></div>
    )}
    {/* No "read this on its own page" link. The introduction was written to be
        the front door of the section, not a separate piece of content, and
        maintaining two copies of the same writing was the mistake. The old
        /why-this-exists/ routes now redirect back here. */}
  </div>

  {/* The rail. A doorway printing whole is a 62ch column of text on a 1280px
      page, which left the right half of For Leaders empty for eight hundred
      pixels. The note that was sitting unused below an empty list moves up
      into that space, taped, with a field of dots behind it: three of the
      design board's own elements, carrying words the room already had. No
      rail is rendered when a page has nothing real to put there. */}
  {!folds && hasRail && (
    <div class="door-rail door-rail-note">
      <Tape color={tone === 'yellow' ? 'coral' : 'yellow'} class="door-tape" />
      <DotGrid cols={7} rows={4} class="door-dots" />
      <slot name="rail" />
    </div>
  )}

  <span class="door-rule" aria-hidden="true"><Mark kind="underline" color={tone} /></span>
</section>
